    "slug": "free-school-in-mudug-offers-education-lifeline-to-pastoralist-children",
    "summary": "A private school in Jarriban, Mudug region, has waived fees for children from drought-affected pastoralist families, allowing them to return to classrooms.",
    "content": "A community school in Jarriban, in Somalia's Mudug region, has begun providing free education to children from pastoralist families who have lost their livestock to drought. The school, which typically charges fees, has waived them for students aged six to 14 from impoverished backgrounds since September, according to Radio Ergo.\n\nThe initiative comes as many families in the region struggle to afford basic education after drought depleted their herds, their primary source of income. Samsam Abdi Mohamed, a mother of three, enrolled her children on 2 October after they had been out of school because she could not pay the $10 monthly fee per child. Her family moved to Jarriban in 2025.\n\nThe free education program targets children who might otherwise remain out of school, offering them a chance to continue their studies. Local observers note that such community-driven efforts are increasingly important as traditional livelihoods face climate-related pressures.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights the ongoing challenges faced by relatives back home and the importance of supporting education initiatives. Many diaspora members send remittances to help with school fees, but drought has stretched family resources thin. The school's decision to waive fees may inspire similar efforts and underscores the need for accessible education in rural areas.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/community-school-in-mudug-supports-free-education-for-children-from-impoverished-pastoralist-families/",
